# Account Sessions with Secure Key Generation
# This test demonstrates secure session key generation

## start a new node
gnoland start

# Test the new secure session commands
! gnokey session
stderr 'manage account sessions'
stderr 'session <subcommand>'

# Test session create help - should show secure key generation
! gnokey session create --help
stderr 'create a new session for an account'
stderr 'session key is generated securely'
stderr '-name'
stderr 'transfer-capacity'
stderr 'validation-only'

# Test session list help
! gnokey session list --help
stderr 'list sessions for an account'
stderr 'identified by the naming pattern'

# Test session revoke help
! gnokey session revoke --help
stderr 'revoke a session from an account'
stderr 'session-key-name'

# Example workflow (these would work with full implementation):

# 1. Create a session with secure key generation
# gnokey session create -account alice -name "daily spending" -transfer-capacity 100ugnot -expiration 24h
# Output would show:
#   Session Name: daily spending
#   Session Key ID: alice_session_1625097600
#   Session Address: g1abc...
#   Session Public Key: pub1...
#   IMPORTANT: The session key has been generated and stored securely

# 2. List sessions to see generated keys
# gnokey session list -account alice
# Output would show:
#   1. Key Name: alice_session_1625097600
#      Address: g1abc...
#      Public Key: pub1...

# 3. Revoke using the generated key name
# gnokey session revoke -account alice -session-key-name alice_session_1625097600

# Benefits of secure generation:
# - Uses cryptographically secure entropy
# - User cannot accidentally provide weak keys
# - Keys are stored safely in keybase
# - Unique naming prevents collisions
# - No private key material exposed to user